speechtext:
Mr. Speaker, in answer to a question put to the Government on Friday last by the hon. member for Lunenburg (Mr. Duff) I promised to give him some further information, and I would now like to read the reply prepared by the Department of Marine and Fisheries in respect to the hon. gentleman's question.
Chapter 33, of the Statutes of 1917 provide that "Cans of lobster shall contain the following minimum amounts of dry lobster meat:-
A one-pound can, fourteen ounces, avoirdupois ;
A three-quater-pound can, ten and one-half ounces, avoirdupois ;
A half-pound can, seven ounces, avoirdupois;
A quarter-pound can, three and one-half ounces, avoirdupois ;
No other size of can shall be packed unless the written permission of the minister has first been obtained."
This seotion came into effect December 15, 1918.
Strong objection was taken to these weights, and following a conference of lobster packers and fishermen held at Halifax in August, 1918, it was decided to introduce a Bill at the coming session to provide weights of 12 ounces, 9 ounces, 6 ounces and 3 ounces of dry lobster meat as legal weights for the above cans.
As the Bill could not be introduced in time for proper notice to be given for the season of 1919 the minister, acting on the power given him by the Act, authorized for that year only the use of the weights proposed in the Bill to be introduced in Parliament.
The Bill came before the House, was referred to the Committee on Marine and Fisheries, and was not approved by the committee. The legislation of 1917, therefore, stands and will be in force in the season of 1920, when the authority given by the minister for 1919 will have lapsed. The note in the Ottawa Journal, quoted by the member for Lunenburg, drew attention to this fact.
The department has notified the packers by circular and issued the information to the press, so that all concerned would have proper knowledge of the state of the law.

No, I am not aware of the fact. And now, Mr. Speaker, I feel that, probably quite unnecessarily, I have detained the House too long in this debate;
I felt that it was my duty to say what I

have said in face of such gratuitous imputations as have been cast upon the honour of a colleague in whom I have the most implicit confidence, and in face-if I may be permitted the expression-in my eyes at least, of the grotesque, the absolutely grotesque, effort to connect that telegram, susceptible of a perfectly innocent interpretation; with every rumour, with every statement, with every letter, anonymous or otherwise, that can be scraped up in regard to the recent elections, and then to say: "We have a prima facie case against the honour of the Prime Minister and of the Minister of the Interior." In face of that grotesque attempt I felt I would not be doing what was just or fair to the distinguished Prime Minister of this country and to my trusted colleague, the Minister of the Interior, if I contented myself with giving a silent vote.

Mr. Speaker, I would like to add a few words in approval of what has been said with reference to the necessity for the extension of the Intercolonial railway in non-railway sections of the Maritime Provinces. In doing so, I speak generally for the province of New Brunswick, and more particularly for that section of the country from which I come.
In 1913, if I remember rightly, a charter was granted by this Parliament for the construction of a railway from Buctouehe, in the county of Kent, to a point in the county of Northumberland, a distance of between fifty-five and sixty miles. The line was surveyed in 1914, but on account of the war nothing more was done. A year or so ago the Government purchased 'the Moncton and Buctouehe railway, which is now part of the Canadian National Railway system. The extension now desired would be from Buc-touche to Chatham or to some other point in Northumberland which would make a satisfactory terminus.
Last winter the municipal council of the county of Kent adopted a resolution asking the Government to take this matter into consideration. The resolution is as follows:
Whereas, the Moncton and Northumberland Strait Railway Company was duly incorporated by an Act of the General Assembly of the Province of New Brunswick in the year 1910, and by the Statutes of Canada in the year 1913, for the purpose of building: a line of railway from Buctouehe in the county of Kent, the terminal of the Moncton and Buctouehe Railway, to a point at or near Loggieville, in the county of Northumberland, N.B.
And, whereas, a survey of the proposed line was duly made in the year 1913, which survey

is now available for reference, and proves it to be an expensivel undertaking, and showing seven miles of road already graded;
And whereas, the Canadian Government has taken over the Moncton and Buctouche Railway, of which the proposed line was to be an extension ;
And, whereas, this proposed line of railway passes through the most thickly populated and richest part of Kent and Northumberland counties, now altogether without railway service, which sections are rich in agricultural, lumber, and Ashing possibilities;
And, whereas, lack of the necessary shipping facilities greatly retard the growth and development of a large portion of these counties;
And, whereas, the question of Anding employment for many of our returned soldiers needs to be solved by the Government;
And, whereas, the building of this railway would give immediate employment to a large number and at the same time open much unused agricultural land for settlement purposes, also be the means of developing our unlimited Ashing facilities along the shores ;
And, whereas, the county of Kent has now cheap electric power available from their Hydro-Electric Light and Power Plant, and the building of this railway would be sure to develop industries of various kinds that are impossible at present owing to lack of railway facilities:
Be it therefore resolved, that this Municipal Council of the Municipality of Kent County, now in annual session assembled, do memorialize the Government of Canada and pray that action may be taken immediately towards the building of this line of railway from Buctouche aforesaid to a point at or near Loggieville in the County of Northumberland, N.B., at the earliest possible time, believing as we do, that such line of railway is in the best interests of the country at large, and especially in the interests of the city of Moncton, county of Kent and county of Northumberland, and thereby serve large sections of country' that have been without railway connection for many years. This service is due these people and the country needs the railway for industrial development. And that a copy of said resolution be forwarded to the Dominion Government, the Boards of Trade at Moncton and Chatham, and to the members of Parliament for the counties of Westmorland, Northumberland, and Kent respectively.
Copies of this resolution have been sent to the Minister of Railways (Hon. J. D. Reid) and to the President of the Canadian National Railway Board, Mr. Hanna. I bring this matter before the House for the purpose of asking the Government to take steps along the lines suggested in this resolution and to have this extension made as soon as possible.
This line would go through many parishes in Kent and Northumberland. Although the route does not always follow the shore, it is nowhere very far from it. If this line was built it would accommodate the whole of the county of Kent from Moncton to Chatham; I therefore urge that the matter be taken into consideration.

That would apply to
another hon. gentleman who sits over there, and who is very particular when it comes to that sort of thing. Twistings and turnings ! It seems so utterly ridiculous for the leader of the Opposition to make such statements, just because his memory is getting weak in his old days and he forgets past history even though the matter dates back only a few months. It is just a couple of months since the Liberals held a convention in the city of Ottawa, just a couple of months since they said:	It is our firm
belief that you ought to reduce the duty oil cloth and to cut down the duty on boots and shoes and other articles of all kinds; it is true that is not what we said in 1911, but a new light has broken upon us since then; this is the policy which we throw to you now; just a new one, that is all, something different from our policy of 1911, just as 1911 was different from 1896 and so on throughout the political history of this country. Every election that has occurred in the Dominion of Canada from Confederation down to the present time has demonstrated this: that as regards the fiscal policy of this country, hon. gentlemen opposite are absolutely as innocent as babes,; they appear to know nothing at all about economic conditions; they appear to be absolutely unable to sense economic things and to get to the roots of them. Every time they have appealed to the people of the Dominion of Canada on a fiscal policy they have been licked, and yet they say: This Government, by waving the flag or something of that sort, has caught the people;

The petition of the undersigned humbly sheweth:
That due to conditions existing by the war, and the desire for the furnishing to the Allied peoples of larger quantities of Canadian butter than was formerly exported, there was admitted freely into the Dominion of Canada, as a .war measure only, a substitute for butter commonly known as Oleomargarine:
And whereas we, the undersigned, believe that Oleomargarine is an inferior product to butter, and is a poor imitation thereof, and is deficient in growth producing substances (particularly in the case of the young), and is not as nourishing to the human body as butter:
Whereas we believe that the continued use of the same would be injurious to the health of the people of this country;
And whereas it is highly essential for the welfare of Canada that a large dairy industry should be made possible in the Dominion;
And whereas we believe that the substitution of oleomargarine for butter will seriously interfere with the development of the dairy industry ;
And whereas tremendous difficulties have arisen in attempting to enforce the pure food laws in the regulation of the manufacture and sale of oleomargarine in countries where this is now permitted, and great expense is being continually incurred in connection with it;
And whereas the dairy industry, properly safeguarded, will be a valuable factor in the reabsorption in civil life of a large number of returned soldiers,
We, your humble petitioners, being residents of the federal district of Antigonish and Guys-borough hereby petition that a strong representation should be made by you at the next session of the Dominion Parliament to the Government to pass legislative measures prohibiting the further importation, sale and manufacture of oleomargarine in or into the Dominion of Canada.
This petition is signed by about fifty inhabitants of that constituency, and other similar petitions have been presented to the Government. If the statements contained in this petition are true, and I do not think they will be contradicted, this Bill should not obtain the assent of the House. We have Boards of Health in Canada, and the question has been raised by my hon. friend (Mr. Sutherland) as to whether this is a wholesome food to be given to our people or not, but so far as we are informed in this discussion, no attention has been given to this matter by our Boards of Health. We also have a Department of Public Health and a staff of officers attached thereto. They should have dealt with this question, and some report should have been made to Parliament as to their attitude on that important phase of it. Investigations have taken place in the United States and I understand they have resulted in the views that have been presented to the committee to-night by the hon. gentleman (Mr. Sutherland). The question of the health of the youth of the country is one of very great importance;
TMr. Sinclair. 1
it is far more important in my view than the financial side of the matter. The raising of a vigorous race of young Canadians is more important to us than the money we should have to pay to the industry that is producing butter. Some one has said: " You canot maintain an A-l empire on a C-3 population." If the use of oleomargarine among the young of the country is going to produce a poorer race of men and women, it is important that the Government should prohibit its consumption. I am not much acquainted with the article myself; I believe it has the appearance of butter of course but is not butter. There was an ingenious Connecticut Yankee once who made a lot of money by manufacturing wooden nutmegs. He thought that if he could produce an article that looked like a nutmeg and sell it cheaply the people would buy it and he would make money. He did succeed for a while in his purpose, but the people soon discovered the fraud. Now the sale of oleomargarine is not altogether as bad as that, but in my opinion it is in the same class. We are endeavouring to produce something that looks like but is not butter-it is an article inferior to it-and we are doing so for the purpose of depressing the price of the real commodity in the market. The hon. gentleman (Mr. Sutherland) during the discussion the other night referred to the fact that the use of colouring is prohibited under regulation of the Government-that the manufacturers of oleomargarine are not allowed to use colouring so as to make the article resemble butter. He also pointed out, however, that the Government have permitted the manufacturers of oleomargarine to purchase highly-coloured butter and to use it in the manufacture of their product; it served the same purpose for them and helped to produce an article which is a good imitation of butter and which can be fraudulently placed on the market and sold ito the consumer for butter.
Now, Sir, I submit, the dairy industry of Canada is a very important one, and that we should not tamper with it in any particular. There should be a thorough investigation before any step like this is attempted to make the use of oleomargarine permanent in the country. The people accepted the Act as a war measure, but the war has now been over for nearly a year, and the fact that the privilege granted during wartime is now being renewed conveys- the impression that -the Government intend to make oleomargarine an article of permanent manufacture and sale in this country.

I object especially to the indirect method of dealing with this question. I say that if the price of butter is too high we ought to deal with the matter fairly and that we should ask our Board of Commerce to investigate the price and regulate it and not endeavour to depress that price by placing upon the market an article that is acknowledged to be inferior. This measure is put forward as being in the interests of the working man. My hon. friend who introduced the Bill told us that there were people in Canada who could not afford to pay for butter at the present price and that those people were to be allowed to purchase oleomargarine. That may be true, but at the Isame time it may not be a good policy 'to adopt. It may be a better policy to raise the wages of the working man so that he and his family could have the benefit of proper food instead of providing that this cheap article should be placed on their table.
I have a suspicion, Mr. Speaker, that some of the big interests are behind this question, that Swift and Company, and some of the large meat packers, who are engaged in the production of tallow and other articles that enter into the manufacture of oleomargarine, have something to do with this question, and that they more than the Canadian consumer, are pressing it. I therefore wish to place on record my opposition to this measure and my support of the views of the hon. member for South Oxford.	'	!
